issue_context.body:
**SDK Version**: 3.1.100

I believe I may have found a bug in either the CSharp ``CodeDomProvider``, or possibly the ``IndentedTextWriter``.

The bug is that when you define a generic method with type parameters in the CodeDom, and render to source code, the resulting source code has an extra blank line after the method start and before the opening brace of the method body, 1 for each type parameter that has been added.

I have a reproduction program, here:

```csharp
using System;
using System.CodeDom;
using System.CodeDom.Compiler;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var codeCompileUnit = new CodeCompileUnit();

        var myNamespace = new CodeNamespace("MyNameSpace");
        codeCompileUnit.Namespaces.Add(myNamespace);

        var classDecl = new CodeTypeDeclaration("MyClass");
        classDecl.Attributes = MemberAttributes.Public;

        myNamespace.Types.Add(classDecl);

        var codeDom = CodeDomProvider.CreateProvider("CSharp");

        for(int numParams = 0; numParams < 6; numParams++)
        {
            var method = new CodeMemberMethod();
            method.Name = "MyMethod";
            method.TypeParameters.AddRange(Enumerable.Range(1, numParams)
                                            .Select(i => new CodeTypeParameter("T" + i))
                                            .ToArray());
            classDecl.Members.Add(method);
        }

        var tw = new IndentedTextWriter(Console.Out, "    ");
        // Generate source code using the code generator.
        codeDom.GenerateCodeFromCompileUnit(codeCompileUnit, tw, new CodeGeneratorOptions
        {
            BlankLinesBetweenMembers = true,
            BracingStyle = "C"
        });
    }
}
```

This yields the following output:
```
namespace MyNameSpace
{


    public class MyClass
    {

        private void MyMethod()
        {
        }

        private void MyMethod<T1>()

        {
        }

        private void MyMethod<T1, T2>()


        {
        }

        private void MyMethod<T1, T2, T3>()



        {
        }

        private void MyMethod<T1, T2, T3, T4>()




        {
        }

        private void MyMethod<T1, T2, T3, T4, T5>()





        {
        }
    }
}
```

It doesn't appear that the ``BracingStyle`` or ``BlankLinesBetweenMembers`` configuration settings have any effect on the behaviour.
